# Copilot and VS Code Local Platform Rules

This addendum applies only to GitHub Copilot and Visual Studio Code (VS Code)
Local agent sessions. Use the shared governance above with the capabilities and
permissions of the actual active session. A model name, endpoint or API key does
not select a host runtime, tool schema, permission grant or execution route.

- Use the actual exposed tool names and argument schemas. Do not translate a
  terminal tool to Bash or PowerShell merely from its name or hook launcher.
- Shared role names identify responsibilities. They do not establish native
  subagent availability or authorize a model call. Keep user approval and quota
  restrictions when choosing an execution route.
- Respect workspace trust, disabled instruction or hook settings, and native
  approval prompts. An installed file or receipt proves neither that rules were
  loaded nor that a hook ran or blocked a tool.
- Use the effective discovered skill source. Preserve higher-priority user or
  other-client skills; report shadowing instead of overwriting them to force
  precedence. Installed copies are outputs, not a second canonical role catalog.
- Keep external execution, state changes and publication under their existing
  shared owners. The selected model and this addendum confer no extra authority.

## Effective agents-mode settings

Resolve settings per key in this order: project `.github/.agents-mode.yaml`,
project legacy `.github/.agents-mode`, user `.copilot/.agents-mode.yaml`, user
legacy `.copilot/.agents-mode`, shared user `.agents-mode.yaml`, then canonical
shared defaults. Do not substitute `.agents`, `.codex` or `.claude` settings.
Reads do not authorize generating or rewriting a missing local configuration.
Project-supplied executable wrapper settings retain the existing first-use
confirmation boundary; another client's global configuration is not confirmation.

This candidate supports only the default home layout. Report a configured
`COPILOT_HOME` as unqualified rather than silently selecting default-home files.
Configuration namespace `copilot` does not authorize a model launch or Codex-native
dispatch. The source resolver can diagnose these layers; its installed general
configuration command and the Codex-specific scalar reminder are not a qualified
Copilot runtime entrypoint.

## Terms and Abbreviations

- API: application programming interface used to access a model or service.
- Host runtime: the application that owns the session, tools and approvals.
- Hook: a host-invoked event handler, separate from instruction-file discovery.
